<script>
import { url } from '@abide/abide'
import Doc from '#src/docs/Doc.abide'
import Code from '#src/docs/Code.abide'
import Example from '#src/docs/Example.abide'
import Section from '#src/docs/Section.abide'
import ContactCard from './ContactCard.abide'
import contactCardSource from './ContactCard.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import Invoice from './Invoice.abide'
import invoiceSource from './Invoice.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import totalSource from './Total.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import initialsSource from '../../../crm-ui/Initials.abide' with { type: 'text' }
</script>

<Doc>
    <Section title="The four spellings">
        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>You write</th><th>What it is</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>&lt;Name/&gt;</code></td><td>an invocation of a component</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>&lt;Tag&gt;…&lt;/Tag&gt;</code></td><td>the same, with children passed into it</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>{'{#component Name(pattern)}'}</code></td><td>a component declared inline, where it is used</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>&lt;slot/&gt;</code> / <code>&lt;slot&gt;fallback&lt;/slot&gt;</code></td><td>where children render, and what renders without them</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A component declared where it is used">
        <p>An inline component is TitleCase like any other, and it exists for the piece of markup
        that is worth naming but not worth a file — a row in one table, a cell shape used twice on
        one page. The parentheses hold the pattern a <code>props()</code> call would destructure,
        and an annotation on it is that call's type argument:</p>

        <Code language="abide" caption="src/contacts/[id]/page.abide — excerpt" code={`{#component Field({ label, value }: { label: string; value: string })}
    <li><span>{label}</span><strong>{value}</strong></li>
{/component}

<ul class="fields">
    <Field label="email" value={contact.email}/>
    <Field label="phone" value={contact.phone}/>
</ul>`} />

        <p>It can be written anywhere markup can: at the top of the file, inside an element, in an
        <code>{'{#if}'}</code> arm or a <code>{'{#for}'}</code> row, or inside another inline
        component. It is invocable anywhere in the block it is written in, before it included, and
        nowhere outside that block.</p>

        <p><strong>It closes over where it is written.</strong> A value the file's
        <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> declares is read by name inside it, and live, the same as
        anywhere else in the template. Declared in a row, it reads that row's item without being
        handed it:</p>

        <Code language="abide" caption="src/contacts/page.abide — excerpt" code={`<script>
import { reactive } from '@abide/abide'
import { listContacts } from '#src/contacts/api.abide'
import type { Contact } from '#src/Contact.ts'

const contacts = listContacts()
let masked = reactive(true)
</script>

<ul>
    {#for contact of contacts by contact.id}
        {#component Phone()}
            <strong>{masked ? '••• ••••' : contact.phone}</strong>
        {/component}
        <li><span>{contact.name}</span><Phone/></li>
    {/for}
</ul>`} />

        <p>Setting <code>masked</code> rewrites each <code>&lt;strong&gt;</code> in place. Its own
        <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> is set up once per invocation, its own
        <code>&lt;style&gt;</code> reaches only what it writes, and the file's
        <code>&lt;style&gt;</code> reaches it too.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Markup a component fills in">
        <p>An inline component written directly between a component's tags is <strong>passed to it
        as a prop</strong> of the same name. The component declares that prop as a function of the
        props it hands over, and invokes it by name:</p>

        <Code language="abide" caption="src/ui/ContactTable.abide" code={`<script>
import { props } from '@abide/abide'
import type { Contact } from '#src/Contact.ts'

const { contacts, Row } = props<{
    contacts: Contact[]
    Row: (props: { contact: Contact }) => unknown
}>()
</script>

<table>
    <tbody>
        {#for contact of contacts by contact.id}
            <tr><Row contact={contact}/></tr>
        {/for}
    </tbody>
</table>
<slot/>`} />

        <Code language="abide" caption="src/contacts/page.abide — excerpt" code={`<ContactTable contacts={contacts}>
    {#component Row({ contact }: { contact: Contact })}
        <td>{contact.name}</td><td>{masked ? '••• ••••' : contact.phone}</td>
    {/component}
    <p>{contacts.length} contacts</p>
</ContactTable>`} />

        <p>The table decides where each row goes and the page decides what is in it, reading its own
        <code>masked</code> as it does. Anything else between the tags is still
        <code>children</code>, for the <code>&lt;slot/&gt;</code>. The passed component is the prop
        and nothing more: it is not in scope anywhere else on the page. <code>abide check</code>
        holds its pattern to the type the table declares, so a row shaped differently is an error at
        the <code>{'{#component}'}</code>.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A prop is a live read">
        <Example
            id="props-live"
            title="`props()` — a prop is a live read, not a snapshot"
            summary="An invoice's line count and the total a child component renders. Press + and watch the child."
            files={{
                'Invoice.abide': invoiceSource,
                'Total.abide': totalSource,
            }}
        >
            <Invoice />
        </Example>

        <p><em>1 declaration, 0 update calls</em> — the arm hands the child a reader and then owns
        the job of telling it when.</p>

        <p><code>props()</code> hands back <strong>one accessor per key</strong>, and a prop
        declared as a plain type is a live read of the caller's expression rather than a snapshot of
        it. So the child re-reads when the parent's value moves, and neither of them arranges that:
        the parent does not call the child again, and the child does not subscribe to anything by
        name.</p>

        <p>You declare every prop as the type of its value, never as a <code>Reactive</code>. A
        child may write one by name, and the caller decides where the write goes: <code>bind:</code>
        sends it to the caller's value, and without <code>bind:</code> it stays in the child until
        the caller's expression changes.</p>

        <p>Destructuring keeps every binding live, and the pattern is <strong>flat</strong>:
        renames, defaults and a rest element, and no nested patterns. The rest element is live too,
        one accessor per key the call site actually passed.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/templates/read-and-write-a-value-by-name')}>Values by name</a> ·
        <a href={url('/templates/bind-a-form-to-state')}>Form binding</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A component another package ships">
        <Example
            id="package-component"
            title="`crm-ui/Initials.abide` — a design system's component, imported by name"
            summary="The CRM's contacts carry the avatar its design system ships. Nothing in the app compiled it, and it renders with its own styles."
            files={{
                'ContactCard.abide': contactCardSource,
                'crm-ui/Initials.abide': initialsSource,
            }}
        >
            <ContactCard />
        </Example>

        <p>A package ships its components as <code>.abide</code> source, and the app imports one
        by the specifier its <code>exports</code> names, the same as a module. It is compiled with
        the app that imports it, wherever it lies: in <code>node_modules</code>, or in a package a
        workspace links from outside the app's root. Its props are typed by the app's project, its
        styles are scoped like the app's own, and it runs on the app's abide rather than on any copy
        the package installed beside it.</p>

        <p>What it is not is an address. A <code>page.abide</code> inside a package is the
        package's own, and a handler a package's <code>&lt;script server&gt;</code> declares has
        no address of its own: the app gives it one by re-exporting it from a
        <code>&lt;script server&gt;</code> of its own, which answers at that file's route.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/templates/scope-styles-to-a-component')}>Styles</a> ·
        <a href={url('/data/read-data-without-writing-an-api')}>Reading data</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A component with no `props` call accepts none">
        <p>There is no implicit bag. A component that never calls <code>props()</code> takes no
        props of its own, and a caller passing one is an error rather than a value that goes
        nowhere. That is the failure this replaces: a typo in a prop name is otherwise a component
        quietly rendering its default.</p>

        <p><code>props()</code> with no type argument hands back an untyped record, and that is the
        opt-out rather than the default. It is also callable from a <code>&lt;script&gt;</code>
        block only: a <code>&lt;script module&gt;</code> block runs once for the module and has no
        instance to read props of, so calling it there is a compile error.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`children` is always accepted and never declared">
        <p>Whatever a caller puts between the tags arrives as <code>children</code>, and
        <code>&lt;slot/&gt;</code> is where it renders.
        <code>&lt;slot&gt;fallback&lt;/slot&gt;</code> renders the fallback where a caller passed
        none, which is how a panel has a default body without the caller having to know what it
        is:</p>

        <Code language="abide" caption="src/ui/Panel.abide" code={`<script>
import { props } from '@abide/abide'

const { title } = props<{ title: string }>()
</script>

<li><span>{title}</span><strong><slot>none yet</slot></strong></li>`} />

        <Code language="abide" caption="src/contacts/[id]/page.abide — excerpt" code={`<ul class="fields">
    <Panel title="phone">+1 415 555 0132</Panel>
    <Panel title="notes"/>
</ul>`} />

        <p><code>children</code> is never declared because declaring it would make it a prop, and
        then a component would have two ways to receive the same thing with different rules.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title={'`{...expr}` spreads, and an explicit prop wins'}>
        <p>A spread on a component spreads props. An <strong>explicit</strong> prop beats a spread
        whatever the source order, so a default set and one override read the same either way round.
        Two spreads against each other resolve in source order, there being nothing else to separate
        them.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/templates/put-a-value-in-the-markup')}>Expressions</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`Attributes<Tag>` types what you pass on to an element">
        <p>A component that wraps a <code>&lt;button&gt;</code> usually declares the few props it
        reads itself and spreads the rest onto the element. <code>Attributes&lt;'button'&gt;</code>
        types that rest as what a <code>&lt;button&gt;</code> takes, so a caller is held to the
        element's own attributes and its <code>onclick</code> is handed a
        <code>MouseEvent</code>:</p>

        <Code language="abide" caption="src/ui/Button.abide" code={`<script>
import { props, type Attributes } from '@abide/abide'

const SIZES = { sm: 'px-2 py-1', md: 'px-3 py-2' }

type Props = { size?: keyof typeof SIZES } & Attributes<'button'>

const { size = 'md', ...attributes } = props<Props>()
</script>

<button class={SIZES[size]} {...attributes}><slot/></button>`} />

        <p><code>&lt;Button type="submit" disabled&gt;</code> checks, and <code>&lt;Button
        type="banana"&gt;</code> does not. The types come from the DOM types TypeScript ships, so
        there is no list of attributes to keep up to date, and a value is typed by what writes the
        attribute rather than by the DOM property behind it: <code>tabindex="-1"</code> and
        <code>{'aria-disabled={true}'}</code> are both fine.</p>

        <p>An SVG element works the same way: <code>Attributes&lt;'svg'&gt;</code> or
        <code>Attributes&lt;'path'&gt;</code> types <code>viewBox</code>, <code>d</code>,
        <code>stroke-width</code> and the rest of SVG's attributes, so an icon component can pass
        them through.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Next">
        <ul>
            <li><a href={url('/templates/run-code-when-a-component-loads')}>Scripts</a> — what runs
            per instance and what runs once</li>
            <li><a href={url('/templates/scope-styles-to-a-component')}>Styles</a> — a component's
            own CSS, scoped to it</li>
            <li><a href={url('/templates/respond-to-a-click')}>Events</a> — why <code>onsave</code>
            on a component is a prop</li>
        </ul>
    </Section>
</Doc>
